=== entry 3de33f774cac
kind: translation
rules: delegate, isolation, background
intent: Each PR owner is an async delegate_task child in its own worktree, since it writes code and the root keeps working while it runs. Keep the owner's full lifecycle (build, open, prove, babysit, merge) and the forge resolution unchanged.
--- upstream
1. **The operator's gates come first.** The grant covers only the queue's items, so items the operator names as theirs and PRs outside the queue stop at merge-ready. A request to state the plan is not a go.
2. **Spawn one owner per PR, and keep its work where a replacement can find it.** Resolve the forge once for the program. GitHub CLI (`gh`) is the default. If `command -v origin` succeeds and Origin can resolve the repository, use `origin pr` for every PR operation. Otherwise stay on `gh` and record the fallback. Never require Graphite (`gt`). Each owner is a Cursor cloud agent that builds, opens its PR per `playbooks/opening-a-pr.md`, proves the change on the real artifact, babysits to green per `playbooks/babysit.md`, and merges once steps 4 and 5 clear it.
--- patched
1. **The operator's gates come first.** The grant covers only the queue's items, so items the operator names as theirs and PRs outside the queue stop at merge-ready. A request to state the plan is not a go.
2. **Spawn one owner per PR, and keep its work where a replacement can find it.** Resolve the forge once for the program. GitHub CLI (`gh`) is the default. If `command -v origin` succeeds and Origin can resolve the repository, use `origin pr` for every PR operation. Otherwise stay on `gh` and record the fallback. Never require Graphite (`gt`). Each owner is a `delegate_task` child with `isolation: "worktree"` and `mode: "async"` that builds, opens its PR per `playbooks/opening-a-pr.md`, proves the change on the real artifact, babysits to green per `playbooks/babysit.md`, and merges once steps 4 and 5 clear it.

=== entry c80ac440daf0
kind: translation
rules: team-kit, invoke
intent: The slop-strip is /deslop when it is installed, with the plugin name dropped. Keep the order: deslop and /no-comments, then rebase, report the code-ready head, then self-proof. The deslop and no-comments skills are named with no slash.
--- upstream
   Open the PR early so CI and bots run alongside the work. The code is ready only after the slop-strip (the `deslop` skill from the `cursor-team-kit` plugin (`/deslop`)) and the `/no-comments` pass, so the full swarm runs on finished code. Then rebase onto current trunk and report the code-ready head before self-proof, so verification runs on the base the patch lands on. Report every later head, and merge-ready once self-proof, CI, and babysit are done. Fix rounds stay on that base unless trunk conflicts or breaks CI, because each rebase restarts CI. Run the repo's checks for the touched paths on the committed head before the code-ready push and before a later push that needs a verifier.
--- patched
   Open the PR early so CI and bots run alongside the work. The code is ready only after the slop-strip (the **deslop** skill, if it is installed) and the **no-comments** pass, so the full swarm runs on finished code. Then rebase onto current trunk and report the code-ready head before self-proof, so verification runs on the base the patch lands on. Report every later head, and merge-ready once self-proof, CI, and babysit are done. Fix rounds stay on that base unless trunk conflicts or breaks CI, because each rebase restarts CI. Run the repo's checks for the touched paths on the committed head before the code-ready push and before a later push that needs a verifier.

=== entry 782fa9a9f78b
kind: translation
rules: store, delegate, isolation, verify-surface, team-kit
intent: The store is the shared per-repo pstack state folder, a spawned agent is recorded by its taskId and childThreadId, and the live lane drives the real surface with the project's verification skill, preview_* tools, or the shell. 'Assume the VM dies' became 'Assume the agent dies' because every child runs on this machine; the point (push early, keep the trail where a replacement finds it) must stay.
--- upstream
   Assume the VM dies. Soon after starting, push and start a decision trail in the root agent's store (path in the system prompt) per the **show-me-your-work** skill, then push after every unit worth keeping. Whoever spawns a subagent records its id, state, and a stuck-after runtime from past runs of its kind in that store, so the root can probe it if the parent dies. Force-push only your own branch, with `--force-with-lease` pinned to the SHA you last pushed or took over, and on any push you did not make, stop and report to the root.
3. **Run owners in parallel off trunk, never stacked,** so each PR lands on its own.
4. **Swarm-verify once.** A full swarm runs once, at the code-ready head. Another full swarm runs only for new behavior, a redesign, or a large new diff. A rebase, a merge-conflict resolution, or a minor fix gets a single verifier at the new head. Fan out independent verifiers at the code-ready SHA per the **swarm** skill. They re-run the gates, prove the load-bearing behavior live on the real surface through its control skill (such as `control-ui` or `control-cli` from `cursor-team-kit`) or a named driver, and audit the diff. The verdict is not clean without the live lane, or before you audit the merge-ready report's receipts, because no lane trusts a claim it has not checked.
--- patched
   Assume the agent dies. Soon after starting, push and start a decision trail in the root agent's store (`~/.local/state/pstack/<repo>/`, where `<repo>` is `basename "$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")"`, created if missing) per the **show-me-your-work** skill, then push after every unit worth keeping. Whoever spawns a subagent records its `taskId`, `childThreadId`, state, and a stuck-after runtime from past runs of its kind in that store, so the root can probe it if the parent dies. Force-push only your own branch, with `--force-with-lease` pinned to the SHA you last pushed or took over, and on any push you did not make, stop and report to the root.
3. **Run owners in parallel off trunk, never stacked,** so each PR lands on its own.
4. **Swarm-verify once.** A full swarm runs once, at the code-ready head. Another full swarm runs only for new behavior, a redesign, or a large new diff. A rebase, a merge-conflict resolution, or a minor fix gets a single verifier at the new head. Fan out independent verifiers at the code-ready SHA per the **swarm** skill. They re-run the gates, prove the load-bearing behavior live on the real surface through the project's own verification skill (otherwise T3's `preview_*` tools for a UI, or the shell for a CLI) or a named driver, and audit the diff. The verdict is not clean without the live lane, or before you audit the merge-ready report's receipts, because no lane trusts a claim it has not checked.

=== entry 94dcced7da44
kind: translation
rules: loop
intent: The hourly audit is an interval schedule_task that is deleted once no delegated work is left. Keep everything each tick does: re-read the playbook, fix drift, probe every agent by side effects, replace stuck agents, ketchup only when something changed.
--- upstream
   On the operator's go, arm `/loop 1h` to audit until no delegated work is left, because long runs drift and agents stall silently. Each tick re-reads this playbook from trunk and fixes drift, such as an unreported head or an unrecorded agent, by having its owner record it, because drift is a gap in the record, not a stall. It probes every agent, down to the leaf lanes, and judges each by its side effects, because status text and summaries can hide a stuck child. An agent is stuck when it errors, cannot start turns, or shows nothing new past its stuck-after runtime, and so is an owner that has neither pushed nor written a trail row by the first tick. One waiting on you or the operator is not stuck, so answer it. Replace a stuck agent at once, without waiting for its stop to land. A tick that changed something for the operator ends by catching them up per the **ketchup** skill, and one that changed nothing stays quiet.
7. **Stand down at once on the operator's stop.** A hold reaches every owner as a zero-writes order. Each reports where it stands and keeps its brief until the operator releases it.
--- patched
   On the operator's go, arm an hourly audit with `schedule_task` (`schedule: {type: "interval", everyMs: 3600000}`), report its `nextRunAt`, and `delete_scheduled_task` once no delegated work is left, because long runs drift and agents stall silently. Each tick re-reads this playbook from trunk and fixes drift, such as an unreported head or an unrecorded agent, by having its owner record it, because drift is a gap in the record, not a stall. It probes every agent, down to the leaf lanes, and judges each by its side effects, because status text and summaries can hide a stuck child. An agent is stuck when it errors, cannot start turns, or shows nothing new past its stuck-after runtime, and so is an owner that has neither pushed nor written a trail row by the first tick. One waiting on you or the operator is not stuck, so answer it. Replace a stuck agent at once, without waiting for its stop to land. A tick that changed something for the operator ends by catching them up per the **ketchup** skill, and one that changed nothing stays quiet.
7. **Stand down at once on the operator's stop.** A hold reaches every owner as a zero-writes order. Each reports where it stands and keeps its brief until the operator releases it.
